# THE PATHS ARE UNANCHORED, AND THAT IS THE FIX
#
# Until 14 Aug 2026 every backend rule here was written the way TEAM-PLAN.md
# §6 draws the map — `/backend/api/feature/auth/` — and 17 of the 20 matched
# nothing at all. The real directory is
#
#     backend/api/src/main/java/com/edunext/edutrack/api/feature/auth/
#
# and CODEOWNERS matches paths, not packages. Only /backend/common/,
# /backend/worker/ and the frontend rules ever resolved; everything else fell
# through to the catch-all, so GitHub requested the lead on every pull request
# and no stream ever saw a change to its own code unless somebody wrote a note
# asking. That is not a theoretical gap — A-037 touched Stream B's
# ResourceController and flagged it for sign-off from a name that is on no
# stream, and nothing caught it because nothing was ever going to.
#
# The patterns below therefore carry no leading slash, so they match at any
# depth. `api/feature/auth/` matches both the main and the test tree, which is
# what you want: a permission test for someone else's feature is their business
# too. The cost is that a pattern could match somewhere unintended, so keep the
# segments long enough to be unambiguous — `feature/auth/` alone would be too
# short, `api/feature/auth/` is not.
#
# To check this file after editing it, confirm each pattern resolves:
#     git ls-files | grep -E 'api/feature/auth/'
# An empty result is a dead rule, and a dead rule is indistinguishable from an
# absent one.
#
# Five frontend rules are deliberately empty today — features/dashboard and
# features/reports (A-054, A-063), features/clients (B-029), features/chat and
# components/ribbon (Stream C and D, unstarted). Those are right paths for
# unwritten work and start protecting it on the commit that creates the
# directory. They are not the same defect as a wrong path, and should not be
# "fixed" by deleting them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

